 ## Overview
-Screen-Space Ray Tracing with Depth Buffer (SSRT) is a technique to trace rays in screen space against a depth buffer to find surface intersections, supporting up to four depth layers per pixel [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently determining ray-surface intersections for effects like reflections, refractions, and ambient occlusion without traversing complex scene graphs or voxel structures [S1, unofficial]. What sets it apart from [Binary Search IsoSurface Extraction](1316-binary-search-isosurface-extraction.md) and [Trilinear Isosurface Intersection](2213-trilinear-isosurface-intersection.md) is that it operates directly on the rendered depth buffer rather than a volumetric or implicit representation, allowing it to capture any geometry already present in the frame buffer [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Ray Tracing](8334-ray-tracing.md) which traverses acceleration structures, SSRT traverses the 2D screen grid, making it highly parallelizable on GPUs but limited to visible surfaces [S1, unofficial].
